-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('be6f3449-9508-5af4-ba78-21ded9c58cc3', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Moths and the Streetlights$q$, $q$Most people notice moths only when the insects circle a porch light. Biologists in the valley town of Esterhollow wanted to know what that habit costs the moths, and whether a change in lighting could help.

In 2021, the town planned to replace its old orange streetlights with bright white LED lamps, which use less electricity. Before the switch, ecologist Kofi Mensah-Larsen set up moth traps along two similar country roads. On the first road, crews installed the standard white lamps. On the second, they installed amber LEDs, which give off very little blue light. The team counted moths on both roads for two summers and also counted the moths visiting flowers in nearby meadows at night.

The results were striking. Traps under the white lamps caught almost twice as many moths as traps under the amber lamps, which meant more insects were being drawn away from the meadows. On the white lamp road, night visits to meadow flowers fell by about a third compared with the year before the switch. On the amber road, flower visits stayed nearly the same. Many plants depend partly on moths to carry pollen at night, so fewer visits may mean fewer seeds over time.

Mensah-Larsen cautions that two roads are a small sample and that weather can change moth numbers from year to year. Still, the town council voted to use amber lamps on all roads that border meadows or woodland. The amber lamps cost slightly more to buy, but they use about the same amount of power as the white ones.$q$, 'draft')
