-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('302f1042-d238-54e1-9bc9-79b37baae6d8', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$What the Date on the Carton Really Means$q$, $q$In the town of Kellerby, a group of high school students in an environmental science class decided to find out how much food their neighbors threw away and why. With help from their teacher, Raymond Achterberg, they surveyed 150 households and asked each family to keep a one-week log of every item discarded.

The students found that the most common reason families gave for throwing food away was the date printed on the package. Many people believed that a "best by" date meant the food became unsafe the next day. In fact, the class learned from the county health office that "best by" usually refers to quality, such as flavor or crispness, rather than safety. Many packaged foods, like crackers or canned beans, remain safe well after that date if stored properly. Foods with a "use by" date, such as some dairy products, deserve closer attention.

The class designed a one-page guide explaining the difference between the labels, with simple tips such as smelling milk before pouring it out and checking cans for dents or bulges. They delivered the guide to the same households and repeated the survey two months later. Families reported throwing away about a quarter less food overall, and items discarded only because of the printed date dropped by nearly half.

Achterberg points out that the second survey depended on families recording honestly, and that some may have paid closer attention simply because they knew they were being watched. The students now hope to give their guide to the town's grocery stores.$q$, 'draft')
